<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kerma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kerma is an archaeological site and ancient city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, the centre of a major Nubian kingdom from around 2500 BCE. It was the first centre of the Kushite state before power shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>. It forms the early background to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerma.jpg" alt="Kerma" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lassi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerma_city.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kerma was the capital city of the Kingdom of Kerma, which had its roots in the pre-Kerma culture of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> from 3500 BCE. It is one of the largest archaeological sites in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and has produced decades of excavation, including thousands of graves and tombs and the residential quarters of the main city around the Western, or Lower, Deffufa. The site was first settled by hunter-gatherers around 8350 BCE. It was mostly abandoned between 5550 and 5150 BCE, possibly because of a decreased flow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, and again between 4050 and 3450 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Agriculture developed around 3000 BCE and a cultural tradition began around Kerma. A state society formed between 2550 and 1550 BCE. The city grew into a large urban centre built around a great adobe temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-deffufa">Western Deffufa</a>, built after 1750 BCE. Copper metallurgy appears from about 2200-2000 BCE. The settlement is divided into Pre-Kerma (c. 3500-2500 BCE), Early Kerma (c. 2500-2050), Middle Kerma (c. 2050-1750), Classic Kerma (c. 1750-1580) and Final Kerma (c. 1580-1500), followed by a late period under Egyptian New Kingdom influence. By 1700 BCE the city had a population of at least 10,000. Its artefacts are marked by blue faience, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kermans</a> learned to work independently of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and by glazed quartzite and architectural inlays.</p><p>The cemetery holds over 30,000 graves, with larger graves ringed by smaller ones, which suggests social stratification. At its southern boundary four burial mounds extend more than 90 metres in diameter and are believed to be the graves of the city's final kings. Egyptian pottery, scarab seals and amulets in the burials point to extensive trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. After the sacking of Kerma, the cemetery was used for the kings of the 25th, or Napatan, dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>George Reisner of Harvard and the Museum of Fine Arts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, led the first major excavations in 1913-1916 and treated Kerma as an Egyptian satellite. Excavations by Charles Bonnet and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> from 1977 to 2003 showed instead a vast independent urban complex that ruled most of the Third Cataract for centuries, with houses, workshops, palaces and a bronze forge. In 2003 Bonnet's team found black granite statues of Twenty-fifth Dynasty pharaohs near Kerma, which are displayed in the Kerma Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kerma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was heir to the earlier <b>Kerma</b> culture, which flourished from around 2500 BCE until its conquest by New Kingdom Egypt around 1500 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A chain of mud-brick fortresses with bastions, ditches and loopholed walls, including Buhen, Mirgissa, Uronarti, Shalfak, Semna and Kumma, controlled the river and the desert routes and watched the growing kingdom of <b>Kerma</b> further south; the Semna Dispatches, copies of reports from these forts, record their patrols.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptian royal statues and monuments looted in <b>Kerma</b>’s raid on Egypt were carried back to Kerma, and the defeat is believed to have been erased later from Egypt’s official records; it is known from an inscription in the tomb of the governor Sobeknakht II at Nekheb.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaeologists divide its ancient history into the A-Group (3700–2800 BCE), C-Group (2300–1600 BCE) and <b>Kerma</b> (2500–1500 BCE) cultures, followed by the age of the New Kingdom, Napata and Meroë.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
